<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Singapura</h1><p class="st-meta">1299 CE – 1398 CE (approx.) · capital Singapura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The 14th-century Malay port kingdom on Singapore island, founded according to legend by Sang Nila Utama and ancestor of the Malacca Sultanate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a> tell how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sang-nila-utama">Sang Nila Utama</a>, a prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, landed on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a>, saw a beast he took for a lion and named the place Singapura, lion city. The register follows the traditional date of 1299. Chinese traveller Wang <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dayuan">Dayuan</a> described Danmaxi, Temasek, around 1330, with a settlement on a hill and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> in the strait.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Excavations on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-canning-hill">Fort Canning Hill</a> and along the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a> River have uncovered Chinese ceramics, gold ornaments and workshops, confirming a busy 14th-century port. Its rulers, called raja, presided over a Malay court and trade in forest goods, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and textiles, under pressure from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and Siam.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1398 the kingdom fell to an attack, attributed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and by Portuguese sources to Siam. The last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Iskandar Shah</a>, also known as Parameswara, fled north and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a> was refounded as a British port in 1819.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-singapura.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Singapura" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Part_of_Singapore_Island_(British_Library_India_Office_Records,_1825,_detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1299 CE – 1398 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Singapura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sang Nila Utama, Iskandar Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/singapore">Singapore</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Singapura, Temasek</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=kingdom-of-singapura" aria-label="Kingdom of Singapura on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7563 2129 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Singapura lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7883 2385L8112 2721M7883 2385L7839 2352"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8112 2721h0M7839 2352h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7883 2385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7883 2385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=kingdom-of-singapura">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
